# Vacuum — packages to keep OUT of the image, even when something depends
# on them. Passed to mklive's -g. Empty by default.
#
# This is the knob for shrinking the ISO, and it is the only knob that
# matters: the desktop is a rounding error next to firmware and the kernel.
# Measured installed sizes from the Void repository:
#
#   linux-firmware-network    438 MB   Wi-Fi and ethernet blobs
#   linux6.18                 166 MB   kernel and modules
#   linux-firmware-nvidia     106 MB   Nvidia GPU
#   sof-firmware               43 MB   Intel audio DSP
#   linux-firmware-amd         31 MB   AMD GPU and CPU microcode
#   glibc-locales              19 MB
#   alsa-firmware              14 MB
#   adwaita-icon-theme         13 MB
#   linux-firmware-intel       13 MB   Intel GPU
#   linux-firmware-broadcom    12 MB
#   dejavu-fonts-ttf           10 MB
#
# That is ~657 MB of firmware alone, against roughly 40 MB for everything
# Vacuum actually puts on screen. None of it costs memory at runtime — the
# kernel loads only the blobs your hardware asks for — so this trades
# download size against how much unknown hardware the image can boot on.
#
# For an image aimed at a machine you already own, dropping the GPU
# firmware for vendors you do not have is free. Dropping
# linux-firmware-network halves the ISO and leaves most laptops with no
# Wi-Fi, which is a bad default for something whose job is to install
# itself onto hardware it has never seen.
#
# Uncomment what you do not need:
# linux-firmware-nvidia
# linux-firmware-amd
# linux-firmware-intel
# linux-firmware-broadcom
# sof-firmware
# alsa-firmware
# memtest86+
# linux-firmware-network
